{
 "id": "M07",
 "kind": "echarts",
 "title": "Thinking length only beats the score where the score has collapsed",
 "section": "appendix",
 "order": 137,
 "caption": "One dot per run that emits hidden reasoning: how well the suspicion score separates backdoored from clean programs across, how well the number of reasoning tokens does it up. Both are areas under the ROC curve, where a half is a coin flip. Above the diagonal, length is the better signal.",
 "notes": [
  "Length wins in 9 of 35 runs and loses in 26. The winners are not a random subset: their suspicion AUC runs 0.592 to 0.753, the bottom of the study. Length rescues a collapsed score rather than beating a working one.",
  "The best-separating arms are far from the diagonal in the other direction. The score is a much stronger signal exactly where the monitor is any good, so this is not a free second opinion.",
  "A monitor that does not reason has no tie-break available at all, so runs reporting no tokens are left out. A zero can mean the model did not think or that the tokens were never recorded, and those two are not distinguishable from the committed tables.",
  "Length is cheap in the sense that it is already paid for, but M20 shows how large that payment is: on some runs the hidden reasoning is most of the bill.",
  "Rebuilt from committed per-sample tables, replacing the exported snapshot. The AUC is the Mann-Whitney statistic with midranks, the same one scripts/analysis_reasoning_tokens.py uses."
 ],
